.el .ds Aq '
.TH iron-vault 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault \- Iron Vault: a local proxy that holds API credentials so the programs that use them never do
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Iron Vault: a local proxy that holds API credentials so the programs that use them never do
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
iron\-vault\-serve(1)
Run the service until SIGTERM or SIGINT
.TP
iron\-vault\-ping(1)
Check that the service is running and show its deployment profile
.TP
iron\-vault\-status(1)
Show the service, vault, approval, memory protection, host unlock outcome and credential states, without secrets. It exits 4 when the service answers and is not Ready
.TP
iron\-vault\-list(1)
List every credential with its kind, permitted origins and standing, never a secret. It needs an open vault, and exits 4 while none is open
.TP
iron\-vault\-unlock(1)
Open the vault: the service prompts for the vault passphrase, read with echo off or from stdin
.TP
iron\-vault\-lock(1)
Clear the vault identity and every cached secret from the service\*(Aqs memory, leaving it Locked
.TP
iron\-vault\-init(1)
Create the vault: the service prompts for the vault and approval passphrases, shows the recovery key once on the controlling terminal, and once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault and approves the initial config as version 1. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked
.TP
iron\-vault\-import(1)
Make the vault from a migration bundle that `export` wrote on another machine. The service prompts for the export passphrase, shows a summary, prompts for a new vault passphrase, shows a new recovery key once on the controlling terminal and, once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault, the source\*(Aqs credentials and approval counters included. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked. The vault routes nothing until you approve a config with `config approve`, at a version above every one the source reserved. The BUNDLE must be a regular file of at most 260 MiB
.TP
iron\-vault\-reset(1)
Discard the vault and the config, so `init` can make a new vault: the way out when no passphrase, recovery key or approver works. It asks for no owner factor, only a confirmation typed at a terminal, which is the vault ID, or the state directory\*(Aqs path when no vault is Initialized. It refuses a pipe, a file or a missing `/dev/tty` with exit code 3 before it connects. The service locks first, then removes the store\*(Aqs files and the config, keeps any foreign file, and ends Locked. Nothing can be recovered afterwards: keep your own copy of the config
.TP
iron\-vault\-export(1)
Write the vault as a migration bundle to BUNDLE, for `import` on another machine. The service checks the vault, prompts for the vault passphrase to authorize the export, then for a new export passphrase, typed twice, which seals the bundle; `import` asks for it. The bundle arrives in `.<BUNDLE name>.partial` beside BUNDLE, created with mode 0600 before anything is sent, and takes BUNDLE\*(Aqs name only once it is whole. An existing BUNDLE or a leftover partial file is refused before anything is sent. The source vault keeps serving afterwards
.TP
iron\-vault\-caller\-token(1)
Issue a fresh caller token: it goes to stdout once, and its token_hash line to stderr. The service keeps no copy; the token works once a config naming the hash is approved with `config approve`
.TP
iron\-vault\-add(1)
Enrol a new credential under NAME; the service prompts for the secret, read with echo off or from stdin, so `pass show fx | iron\-vault add fx \-\-origin api.fx.example` works. It never overwrites an existing name
.TP
iron\-vault\-replace(1)
Replace NAME\*(Aqs record with a new one, and with it the kind and the origins; the old record need not be readable, so this also repairs a damaged one. The service shows what changes, asks for the approval passphrase as owner presence, then prompts for the new secret. On stdin, the first line is the approval passphrase and the rest the secret. Routes that use NAME keep working only if the new kind and origins suit them; otherwise nothing changes
.TP
iron\-vault\-rm(1)
Remove the credential NAME after you type the approval passphrase. It refuses while a route of the active config uses the name: approve a config without those routes first
.TP
iron\-vault\-config(1)
Change the approved configuration
.TP
iron\-vault\-recovery(1)
Check the owner\*(Aqs offline recovery key
.TP
iron\-vault\-recipient(1)
Manage the keys that can open the vault
.TP
iron\-vault\-approver(1)
Manage the approvers that sign configuration approvals
.TP
iron\-vault\-forget\-machine(1)
Check that a retired machine\*(Aqs recipient key is gone from the vault, then print how to decommission the machine. Run it on the vault that stays, with the key `recipient list` showed for the machine. It exits 1 with `machine\-not\-forgotten`, telling you to run `recipient remove`, while the key is still enrolled. It warns when the vault no longer meets the independence rule or no recovery check has run lately. It changes nothing and runs no TPM tool: you run every step it prints
.TP
iron\-vault\-completions(1)
Print a shell completion script to stdout. It never contacts the service, so it also runs at build time
.SH EXTRA
Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  the service answered with an error
  2  usage error
  3  no answer from the service: it is not running, this account may not connect, the exchange failed, a prompt
     got no value, or a secret value the service sent could not be shown on a terminal; `reset` and `import` also
     exit 3, before they connect, when stdin is not a terminal or /dev/tty cannot be opened; `export` also
     exits 3 when the bundle it received is incomplete or cannot be kept
  4  `status`: the service answered, and it is not Ready; `list`: no vault is open, so the credentials are unknown
